<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
ASP.NET forms authentication keeps the identity of the signed-in user in an authentication ticket,
which is sent to the browser in a cookie. The <code>protection</code> attribute of the
<code>&lt;forms&gt;</code> element in <code>Web.config</code> controls how the ticket is protected.
The value <code>None</code> turns off both encryption and validation, so anyone who can set the
cookie can create or change a ticket and act as any user, including an administrator.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Use <code>protection="All"</code>, or remove the <code>protection</code> attribute because
<code>All</code> is the default. It encrypts the ticket and validates it with a message
authentication code.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following configuration turns off the protection of the forms authentication ticket:
</p>
<sample language="xml"><![CDATA[<configuration>
  <system.web>
    <authentication mode="Forms">
      <forms loginUrl="~/Account/Login" protection="None" />
    </authentication>
  </system.web>
</configuration>]]></sample>

<p>
The following configuration encrypts and validates the ticket:
</p>
<sample language="xml"><![CDATA[<configuration>
  <system.web>
    <authentication mode="Forms">
      <forms loginUrl="~/Account/Login" protection="All" />
    </authentication>
  </system.web>
</configuration>]]></sample>
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<ul>
<li>
Only <code>Web.config</code> files and <code>Web.&lt;name&gt;.config</code> transformation files are
examined. A copy under another name, and an application configuration file such as
<code>Program.exe.config</code>, are not used for the settings of a web application and are not
reported.
</li>
<li>
A <code>&lt;forms&gt;</code> element is reported only when the <code>mode</code> of its
<code>&lt;authentication&gt;</code> element is <code>Forms</code>. The default mode is
<code>Windows</code>, and ASP.NET does not use forms authentication in the other modes. A mode that
a parent configuration sets is not considered.
</li>
<li>
The runtime accepts only the exact spelling <code>None</code>. Other spellings, such as
<code>none</code>, make the configuration invalid, so they are not reported.
</li>
<li>
Configuration inheritance and transformations are not evaluated. A value that a transformation file,
such as <code>Web.Release.config</code>, overrides at publish time is still reported in the base
configuration file, and a transformation file that sets the value is reported on its own.
</li>
<li>
The documentation of the <code>protection</code> attribute allows <code>None</code> for sites that
use cookies only for personalization and have less stringent security requirements. Such a site is
reported too.
</li>
</ul>
</section>

<references>
<li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/dotnet/netframework-4.0/1d3t3c61(v=vs.100)">forms Element for authentication (ASP.NET Settings Schema)</a>.</li>
<li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/dotnet/netframework-4.0/532aee0e(v=vs.100)">authentication Element (ASP.NET Settings Schema)</a>.</li>
<li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.web.configuration.formsprotectionenum">FormsProtectionEnum Enum</a>.</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
